<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>Harness.Cleanup</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>harness-cleanup</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    DB-7's ENTRY POINT. `Harness.Results/Cleanup.cs` has computed cleanup eligibility since it was
    written and NOTHING CALLED IT: zero references anywhere in src/ outside its own file and its
    tests. A library nobody can run is a design that has not been tested against reality, and the
    first thing running it against the real corpus found was that a tag table and a UDT are
    STRUCTURALLY OUTSIDE X-J's ownership rule (see CleanupRun's UNADDRESSABLE section) — which no
    unit test over hand-made candidates could have surfaced.

    *** THIS BINARY PLANS. IT CANNOT DELETE, AND THE CONFIRMATION FLAGS ARE REFUSED BY NAME. ***

    That is `download-plan`'s shape, chosen for `download-plan`'s reason: the delete verb lives in
    `openness-cli delete` (net48, Portal, its own fences), and a second path to the same destructive
    act is a second fence to keep correct. What this emits instead is the exact command line per
    removal, so the plan is executable by a party that holds the Portal token — and CleanupStructure
    Tests walks this compiled assembly asserting no file-mutating or process-launching call exists in
    it, with a denominator and a live positive control, because a claim about an assembly needs a
    walk over the assembly.

    It consumes ARTIFACTS and never invokes a tool: `converter cross-check` in JSON form,
    `converter claims` in JSON form, the vector files, the drain report. Same reason Harness.Results opens
    no socket — provenance is then visible on the command line, and the binary stays testable with
    no converter, no Portal and no rig in the room.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Harness.Results\Harness.Results.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
